{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfryy9nEQA4q1r5fWxn5Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`resolver/solve.py` is implemented and all 25 tests in `tests/test_solve.py` pass under both pytest and unittest. I did not commit anything.\n\n**What it does**\n- **`Version`**: dotted integers compared numerically, with trailing zeros ignored (`1.0 == 1`). It is hashable and `str()` returns the original text.\n- **`Constraint.parse`**: handles bare names, comma-separated clauses, all six operators, and whitespace. Malformed input raises `ValueError`. `.name` and `.allows(Version)` are provided.\n- **`resolve`**: a backtracking search that decides the alphabetically first unassigned required package first and tries its highest version first. Each choice adds that version's requirements, and forward checking fails early if any pending package runs out of candidates. Cycles and self-dependencies are handled because already-assigned packages are checked against new constraints rather than re-decided.\n- **Errors**: `ResolutionError` names the package where the search first dead-ended, for example `b` in the spec's `a requires b<1` case.\n\n**Tests** (`tests/test_solve.py`)\n- Version and constraint semantics, including whitespace and invalid input.\n- The spec example, highest-version preference, transitive dependencies, unreachable packages excluded, cycles, self-dependencies, and backtracking out of a dead end.\n- Error messages name the leaf package, not the root.\n- A 300-version index that is both unsatisfiable and satisfiable; both finish in milliseconds.\n- A seeded randomized cross-check (300 trials). It compares against brute-force enumeration for validity, for `ResolutionError` exactly when no solution exists, and for lexicographic maximality among solutions with the same package set. It covered 231 solvable and 69 unsolvable cases.\n\n**Interpretation choices to check**\n- \"Lexicographically greatest\" is implemented as the greedy order above. When two valid solutions pick different package sets, the tie is broken by that search order, not by a stricter comparison over the union of packages. The spec does not pin this case down.\n- Which package is named in an error when several dead-ends occur is \"the first one the search hits\". Other readings are possible.\n\nI did not add a timing guarantee for adversarial indexes beyond forward checking; the spec says that is sufficient."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2110,"cache_read_input_tokens":68252,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2110},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4c880d80-f1e1-4792-96bb-2f22222b57d3","uuid":"32c80a11-a549-4281-b7db-608f48a9282b","timestamp":"2026-10-09T16:31:41.364Z","request_id":"req_011Cfryy9W7MPMvYVQpcUFe8"}
